  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 4♥. South must lose tricks to the ♥A and ♣A. West's spade lead has created a third certain loser. South must try to avoid losing a diamond trick.\n\nIf the ♠A is played at trick 1, the contract will be in trouble. If South tries to knock out the ♥A and ♣A, the defense will play diamonds creating a fourth inescapable loser.\n\nIf South instead plays a second round of spades, West will win and switch to diamonds. Dummy will have an established spade winner to take care of South's ♦2, but the dummy cannot be reached without losing the lead.\n\nSouth does better to let West win at trick one. West will switch to diamonds as before, but South is in control. South can win the ♦A and finesse in spades. The ♠A will take care of South's ♦2 and South's only remaining losers will be the ♥A and the ♣A.",
